Q: What is the purpose of the pagingRange element as stated in the SFC-04 specification?
A: According to the SFC-04 specification, the **pagingRange** element (`SearchEmployerRequestType/pagingRange/dgt:PagingRangeType`) is used to **request paging by passing the first row and page size**. Specifically:

- It allows the `searchEmployer` service to **return a subset of results** that meet the specified search criteria, rather than all matching records at once.
- It lets the caller **specify how many results should be returned per page** and **the index of the first row to return**.
- It also **returns the total number of results** meeting the search criteria (so callers know how many pages exist).
- It is **optional** in the request — if not provided, the default is **20 results per page beginning at index 1**, with results ordered by the Employer Number (`sloc_code`).
- It also appears in the response (`SearchEmployerResponseType/pagingRange`) to echo back the paging parameters used and the total results found.

Citation: Wiki: RLSI-6059 searchEmployer SBS to support pagination § SFC-04 pagingRange added to searchEmployer SBS
----------------------------------------
Q: Why was pagination added to the searchEmployer operation?
A: Pagination (the new optional `pagingRange` element) was added to the **searchEmployer** SBS operation to support higher Employer search volumes and improve performance, usability, and response management. Specifically, the rationale was to:

- **Ensure consistent, ordered results** — results are returned in a defined order (sorted by Employer Number), improving data clarity and predictability.
- **Provide visibility of total matches** — the response now returns the total number of records meeting the search criteria.
- **Improve performance** — results are returned in manageable subsets/pages rather than one large payload, optimizing system performance and reducing response time.
- **Give callers flexibility** — users can specify the number of records per page (`resultsPerPage`) based on business needs.
- **Support incremental retrieval** — users can define the starting index (`firstResult`) so that, together with page size, the full result set can be retrieved incrementally and efficiently.

This was driven by client requirement RLSI-6059 (Royal London), which needed `searchEmployer` to scale for higher Employer volumes and align with Workplace Pension/HMRC requirements. If no `pagingRange` is supplied, the default is 20 results per page starting at index 1.

Citations:
- Jira: BASE-458911 — Sonata Business Services (SBS) - Operation searchEmployer added with optional element pagingRange (type PagingRangeType) to support pagination for higher Employer volumes, ensuring search flexibility
- Jira: BASE-458836 — Sonata Business Service (SBS) - Operation searchEmployer now supports pagination & indexed sorting via new optional pagingRange element, improving scalability for Workplace Pension product, ensuring alignment with HMRC requirements
- Wiki: RLSI-6059 searchEmployer SBS to support pagination § High Level Solutions
- Wiki: RLSI-6059 searchEmployer SBS to support pagination § SFC-04 pagingRange added to searchEmployer SBS
----------------------------------------
Q: Why is the pagingRange element optional rather than mandatory in the searchEmployer request?
A: The `pagingRange` element is optional (Min: 0, Max: 1) in the `searchEmployer` request because the service is designed to work with sensible pagination defaults when the caller doesn't need to control paging explicitly. Specifically:

- If `pagingRange` is not provided, the search still returns results, defaulting to **20 results per page starting at index 1**, ordered by Employer Number (sloc_code).
- The element exists to let SBS users *optionally* override this default — specifying how many results per page (`resultsPerPage`) and which row to start from (`firstResult`) — for cases where they want to page through large result sets incrementally.
- This is confirmed by the acceptance criteria: running the SBS without `pagingRange` on ≤20 matching employers returns all of them (AC-4), and without it on >20 matching employers returns the first 20 by default (AC-5) — showing the request functions correctly whether or not the tag is supplied.

So making it optional avoids forcing every caller to specify pagination parameters just to get a default, reasonable result set, while still allowing explicit control when needed.

Citation: Wiki: RLSI-6059 searchEmployer SBS to support pagination § SFC-04 pagingRange added to searchEmployer SBS
----------------------------------------
Q: What did the searchEmployer operation return before the pagination enhancement?
A: Before the pagination enhancement, the `searchEmployer` SBS had no `pagingRange` element at all — it simply returned **all** Employers matching the search criteria in a single, unordered response, with no way to limit results per page, no starting index control, and no indication of the total number of matching records. This is confirmed by the enhancement's stated rationale, which lists what was previously missing:

- No defined/consistent order for results
- No visibility of the total number of matching records
- No ability to return results in manageable subsets (results came back as one large payload)
- No way for the caller to specify records per page
- No way for the caller to set a starting index for incremental retrieval

(Wiki: RLSI-6059 searchEmployer SBS to support pagination § Solution Overview / High Level Solutions — HLS-02 "Supporting pagination in Employer search (RLSI-6059)")

Related delivery ticket confirms the same "before" state and the fix: *"Sonata Business Service (SBS) operation searchEmployer has been enhanced with a new element pagingRange... The rational behind this change is to: Ensure results are returned in a defined and consistent order... Provide visibility of total number of matching records... Return results in manageable subsets rather than a single large payload..."* (Jira: BASE-458911 — Sonata Business Services (SBS) - Operation searchEmployer added with optional element pagingRange (type PagingRangeType) to support pagination for higher Employer volumes, ensuring search flexibility)

In short: previously, `searchEmployer` returned the **complete, unpaginated set** of matching Employers in one response, with no ordering guarantee and no total-count metadata — a problem for performance/usability once Employer volumes grew large (e.g. for the Workplace Pension product).
----------------------------------------
Q: What changed in the searchEmployer request schema in Sonata 16.2?
A: Based on Jira and wiki content for Sonata 16.2, the `searchEmployer` request schema changed as follows:

**New optional `pagingRange` element added (type `PagingRangeType`)**
- Added to both `SearchEmployerRequestType` and `SearchEmployerResponseType` (min 0, max 1 occurrence).
- In the **request**, it lets callers specify pagination by passing the first result index (`firstResult`) and the page size (`resultsPerPage`).
- If `pagingRange` is **not provided**, the service defaults to returning 20 results per page, starting at result index 1.
- Results are ordered by **Employer Number** (`sloc_code`).
- The corresponding **response** echoes back `pagingRange` and adds `totalResults`, giving visibility into the total number of records matching the search criteria — so a large result set can be retrieved incrementally across multiple calls.

This was delivered to improve performance, usability, and response management for higher employer volumes (relevant to Workplace Pension/HMRC-scale searches), and it also underpins related work to return multiple employers when searching by a non-unique Employer External Reference.

Citations:
- Jira: BASE-458911 — Sonata Business Services (SBS) - Operation searchEmployer added with optional element pagingRange (type PagingRangeType) to support pagination for higher Employer volumes, ensuring search flexibility
- Jira: BASE-458836 — Sonata Business Service (SBS) - Operation searchEmployer now supports pagination & indexed sorting via new optional pagingRange element, improving scalability for Workplace Pension product, ensuring alignment with HMRC requirements
- Jira: FEAT-9707 — RLSI-6059 - searchEmployer SBS to support pagination and return employer external refs
- Wiki: RLSI-6059 searchEmployer SBS to support pagination § SFC-04 pagingRange added to searchEmployer SBS

Note: my `jira_version_range` tool call (16.1→16.2 and 16.0→16.2) returned no results — version labels in this data appear as "Sonata 16. 2" (with a space), which may not match the tool's expected format, so I relied on direct Jira search instead.
----------------------------------------
Q: Would a client that calls searchEmployer without pagingRange see a change in behaviour?
A: Yes — behaviour changes, but the change is backward-compatible in that no request modification is required, only the response content shifts.

- **pagingRange is optional** in the `searchEmployer` request — a client that doesn't send it won't get a validation error. (Wiki: RLSI-6059 searchEmployer SBS to support pagination § SFC-04 pagingRange added to searchEmployer SBS)
- If pagingRange is omitted, the SBS now **defaults to 20 results per page, starting at index 1**, whereas previously (per the acceptance criteria) all matching employers were presumably returned unpaginated:
  - **AC-4**: If ≤20 employers match, all are returned (no visible change).
  - **AC-5**: If **more than 20** employers match, only the **first 20** are now returned by default (with the default pagination setting), sorted by Employer Number, along with a total-results count — so a client relying on getting *all* matching employers in one call **will now silently receive a truncated (first-page) result set** if more than 20 match. (Wiki: RLSI-6059 searchEmployer SBS to support pagination § SFC-04 pagingRange added to searchEmployer SBS)
- Additionally, results are now **always sorted by Employer Number (sloc_code)**, and the response includes the total number of results meeting the search criteria — both new aspects of behaviour even without the client supplying pagingRange. (Wiki: RLSI-6059 searchEmployer SBS to support pagination § SFC-04 pagingRange added to searchEmployer SBS)
- This is confirmed in Jira: "*pagingRange* is an optional element in request/response... The default value to return 20 records per page, starting from index 1." (Jira: BASE-460238 — RLSI-6059 searchEmployer SBS to support pagination and return multiple results when searching by External Reference)

**Bottom line:** The call itself won't break, but if a client's search criteria matches more than 20 employers, it will now only get the first 20 (sorted by Employer Number) instead of the full result set, plus a new total-results indicator in the response — a functional behaviour change to watch for during upgrade testing.
----------------------------------------
Q: What originally-planned objectives were dropped from the searchEmployer enhancement scope?
A: Based on the wiki documentation for the **RLSI-6059 searchEmployer SBS to support pagination** enhancement, several originally-planned objectives were dropped (shown as struck-through/cancelled in the page):

1. **Searching Employer by multiple Employer External References** – the original plan included allowing a request to search using multiple Employer External References (current API only supports a single reference per request), but this was moved to "Out of Scope" (Wiki: RLSI-6059 searchEmployer SBS to support pagination § Out of Scope).

2. **Returning multiple Employer External References in the Search Employer response** – originally planned so the API could return more than one External Reference type per employer (currently limited by the single "Employer Reference Template" setting), but this too was placed in "Out of Scope" (Wiki: RLSI-6059 searchEmployer SBS to support pagination § Out of Scope).

3. **HLS-01 "Returning all Employer External References in Employer search" (RLSI-6149)** – a full high-level solution originally proposed to change searchEmployer SBS logic so it could return multiple employers with the same non-unique External Reference value, and to support returning multiple reference types — this entire solution item is struck through, indicating it was dropped from delivered scope (Wiki: RLSI-6059 searchEmployer SBS to support pagination § High Level Solutions).

4. Correspondingly, in the **Solution Overview**, the objectives "searching by Employer External Reference" and "returning multiple Employers meeting search criteria when search is run by an External Reference Value" are both struck through, leaving only **pagination support** as the delivered objective (Wiki: RLSI-6059 searchEmployer SBS to support pagination § Solution Overview).

**In summary:** the enhancement was originally scoped to add pagination *and* improve External Reference search/return capability (multi-value search and multi-reference-type return), but only the **pagination** capability was ultimately delivered — the External Reference-related objectives were dropped from scope.
----------------------------------------
Q: What does the searchEmployer response echo when a client requests a page starting at result 51 with 50 results per page and 80 employers match?
A: This is exactly the scenario in **AC-7** of the pagination spec (80 employers total, firstResult=51, resultsPerPage=50):

- **30 Employers are returned**, starting with the 51st result (since only 80 exist, results 51–80 are returned).
- Employers are sorted by the Employer Number.
- The response echoes back:
  - `pagingRange/firstResult = 51`
  - `pagingRange/resultsPerPage = 50`
  - `pagingRange/totalResults = 80`

Citation: Wiki: RLSI-6059 searchEmployer SBS to support pagination § SFC-04 pagingRange added to searchEmployer SBS (AC-7)
----------------------------------------
Q: Does the searchEmployer enhancement support searching by Employer External Reference?
A: Yes. The **searchEmployer** SBS already supports searching by Employer External Reference — this was implemented as far back as Sonata 6.0 (Jira: BASE-112902 — SchemeService.searchEmployer - implement searching by employer external reference).

Key details of how it works:

- **Single reference search**: searchEmployer supports searching by a single Employer External Reference and returning a single External Reference. The type of external reference returned is controlled by the *Employer Reference Template* field under *Common > Schemes > Scheme Structure > Scheme Level Maintenance > Scheme* (Wiki: RLSI-6059 searchEmployer SBS to support pagination § ~~SEF-01 Employer External Reference in searchEmployer SBS~~).
- **Matching type constraint**: Returning multiple Employers with the same External Reference value is only possible if the external reference passed in the request matches the type selected in the *Employer Reference Template* — i.e., the Platform Operator must pre-configure which reference type is searchable, and that same type is used for both the request and the response (Wiki: RLSI-6059 searchEmployer SBS to support pagination § ~~SFC-02 Enhance searchEmployer SBS to return multiple Employers when search by Employer External Reference.~~).
- **Non-unique references / multiple results**: Originally, if multiple Employers shared the same non-unique External Reference value, the search would fail with error *"BRA-151053 - Cannot find a unique scheme location..."*. This was enhanced (Jira: RLSI-6149 — searchEmployer SBS to return multiple Employers by non-unique external reference) so the validation restricting results to a single match was removed specifically for searchEmployer, allowing multiple matching Employers to be returned when searching by a non-unique External Reference.
- **Limitation (out of scope)**: Searching by *multiple* different Employer External References in a single request is **not** supported — only one Employer External Reference value can be passed per request (Wiki: RLSI-6059 searchEmployer SBS to support pagination § Out of Scope).
- **Pagination**: More recently, `pagingRange` was added to searchEmployer to paginate results (default 20 records per page) when a search — including one by non-unique External Reference — returns multiple Employers (Jira: BASE-460238 — RLSI-6059 searchEmployer SBS to support pagination and return multiple results when searching by External Reference).
- Related historical defects confirm this capability has existed and been fixed over time when broken, e.g. Jira: DISCO-924 — Unable to search for employer via SBS using external reference, and Jira: BASE-195626 — Unable to search for employer via SBS using external reference.

**Summary**: Yes, searchEmployer supports searching by Employer External Reference, but only one reference (of the type configured in the Employer Reference Template) per request; multiple Employers can be returned if that reference is non-unique, with pagination support added later.
----------------------------------------
Q: Does the searchEmployer pagination change return Employer External References in the response?
A: ## Answer: No — the pagination change was descoped from returning Employer External References

The pagination enhancement to `searchEmployer` (RLSI-6059 / FEAT-9707 / BASE-458911) delivered only the `pagingRange` element for paging (default 20 records per page, starting at index 1). It does **not** deliver the return of multiple/employer external references.

Key evidence:

- The wiki design page's **Objectives** section shows the "return Employer External References" objective struck through (descoped), leaving only: "To be able to divide into pages the results returned by the Employer search API." (Wiki: RLSI-6059 searchEmployer SBS to support pagination § Objectives)
- **Out of Scope** explicitly lists (struck through, i.e. confirmed out of scope): "Returning multiple Employer External References in Search Employer API — Current Sonata API allows returning a single Employer External Reference by Search Employer... configured in Employer Reference Template field in Scheme details." (Wiki: RLSI-6059 searchEmployer SBS to support pagination § Out of Scope)
- **High Level Solutions** confirms this work was split off to a separate initiative — "Returning Employer External References in Employer search (RLSI-6149)" is struck through and marked as delivered under a different ticket, while HLS-02 "Supporting pagination in Employer search (RLSI-6059)" remains the actual scope of this change. (Wiki: RLSI-6059 searchEmployer SBS to support pagination § High Level Solutions)
- Jira confirms this split: RLSI-6059's description notes "Requirements related to searching by employer external reference has been moved to RLSI-6149." (Jira: RLSI-6059 — searchEmployer SBS to support pagination)
- The schema change ticket BASE-458911 confirms only the `pagingRange` element (type `PagingRangeType`) was added to `searchEmployer` for this change — no external reference field is mentioned as added. (Jira: BASE-458911 — Sonata Business Services (SBS) - Operation searchEmployer added with optional element pagingRange...)

So, if you need multiple/employer external references returned in `searchEmployer` search results, that is covered under the separate ticket **RLSI-6149**, not this pagination change.
----------------------------------------
Q: Does the pagingRange element apply to the createEmployerAccount operation?
A: No — the `pagingRange` element applies only to the **searchEmployer** operation, not `createEmployerAccount`. It was added to `SearchEmployerRequestType/pagingRange` and `SearchEmployerResponseType/pagingRange` to support paging of search results (optional, defaults to 20 results per page ordered by Employer Number). `createEmployerAccount` appears only in the regression criteria as one of several other SBS operations that should be regression-tested when this change is made — it's not itself a paginated search operation and has no `pagingRange` element defined for it.

Citation: Wiki: RLSI-6059 searchEmployer SBS to support pagination § SFC-04 pagingRange added to searchEmployer SBS
